1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,220
Did you know one trip to hospital can

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:04,220
mean getting more than one bill?

3
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:05,740
Let's find out why!

4
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:09,100
Imagine throwing the best birthday party ever.

5
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:10,500
You need a place to have it,

6
00:00:10,520 --> 00:00:12,380
and a DJ to play the music.

7
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,380
A hospital stay is the same.

8
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:15,940
The hospital is the venue,

9
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:17,820
and the doctor is the DJ.

10
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,700
The venue charges for the room,

11
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:21,420
the tables and the food.

12
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:23,380
The hospital charges for your bed,

13
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:26,300
the operating room, your meals and the nurses.

14
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:28,580
The DJ doesn't work for the venue,

15
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:30,380
so they send their own bill.

16
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:33,060
In hospital, your surgeon and your anaesthetist,

17
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:34,660
the doctor who helps you sleep,

18
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:36,380
can each send a bill too.

19
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:38,620
Private health insurance is like a party fund

20
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:39,860
you save into.

21
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:42,140
Hospital cover usually pays most of the venue

22
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:42,860
bill.

23
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:44,860
You might pay a small amount first,

24
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:46,220
called an excess.

25
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:48,620
For doctors, the government has a price list

26
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:50,700
called the Medicare schedule fee.

27
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:52,540
Think of it as the official price for

28
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:53,500
each song.

29
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:56,700
Medicare pays 75 dollars of every 100 on

30
00:00:56,720 --> 00:00:57,580
that list.

31
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:00,020
Your health fund pays the other 25.

32
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:02,580
Together, they cover the official price.

33
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:04,860
But DJs can set their own price!

34
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:06,620
If they charge more than the list,

35
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:08,460
the extra bit is called the gap,

36
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:10,220
and you might have to pay it.

37
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:11,900
Here's something important.

38
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:13,900
Everything so far works the same no matter

39
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:15,740
which health fund you choose.

40
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:18,820
Every fund in Australia follows these same rules.

41
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:21,020
Where funds are different is their gap deals

42
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:22,140
with doctors.

43
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:24,380
With a deal, you pay nothing extra,

44
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:26,180
or a small amount you know about ahead

45
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:27,020
of time.

46
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:28,220
Wait, what?

47
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:30,260
Usually we cheer for the little guy.

48
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:33,180
But here, more gap deals means smaller gaps

49
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:34,140
for you.

50
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:36,460
Big funds, or small funds that team up

51
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:38,060
in a big shared network,

52
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:39,927
usually have the most deals.

53
00:01:39,947 --> 00:01:41,660
And here's a tip for grown-ups.

54
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:44,500
Don't assume a not-for-profit fund will always give

55
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:46,060
more back to its members.

56
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:48,700
Some do, but some for-profit funds give back

57
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:49,340
more.

58
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:51,367
It's always worth comparing.

59
00:01:51,387 --> 00:01:52,300
So remember:

60
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:53,793
the hospital is the venue,

61
00:01:53,813 --> 00:01:55,740
and the doctor is the DJ.

62
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:57,740
Ask each doctor what they charge,

63
00:01:57,760 --> 00:02:01,940
and check your fund's gap deals at privatehealth.gov.au.

64
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:02,860
Next time:

65
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:04,780
what actually happens in hospital?

66
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:06,220
Why go private at all?

67
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:07,820
What are waiting periods?

68
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:10,460
And my surgeon also operated on my next-door

69
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:13,100
neighbour, who doesn't have private health insurance.

70
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:15,060
So what's the difference?
